WEBVTT

00:02.700 --> 00:04.701
(panting)

00:15.883 --> 00:18.169
(clattering)

00:22.058 --> 00:24.059
(indistinct chatter
and laughter)

00:26.063 --> 00:27.880
It's...

00:27.915 --> 00:29.933
♪

00:58.486 --> 01:00.054
(passing gas)

01:02.591 --> 01:05.277
Chris, would you like
to read your personal essay?

01:05.312 --> 01:06.913
Okay, Mrs. Donovan.

01:08.399 --> 01:11.252
"Following Mrs. Donovan"
by Chris Griffin.

01:11.286 --> 01:13.437
"The first thing that
Mrs. Donovan does

01:13.472 --> 01:15.140
"when she gets
home from school

01:15.174 --> 01:16.592
"is put on sweatpants.

01:16.626 --> 01:18.877
"Then she eats a whole
tube of cookie dough,

01:18.911 --> 01:20.546
"but she must be
allergic to it

01:20.581 --> 01:22.699
"because there are tears
coming out of her eyes

01:22.733 --> 01:24.735
"and she always
throws up right after.

01:24.769 --> 01:26.553
"Then she puts
paper doll clothes

01:26.588 --> 01:28.323
on a picture
of a sonogram."

01:28.357 --> 01:29.658
Chris, I think
you should stop.

01:29.692 --> 01:31.827
What? I'm doing
the best I can.

01:31.862 --> 01:33.763
I'm not Nathaniel
Hawthorne.

01:33.797 --> 01:35.766
(chuckling)

01:35.800 --> 01:37.551
Nathaniel,
what are you doing?

01:37.585 --> 01:39.954
Just ruining ninth
grade for everyone.

01:42.992 --> 01:45.060
Mr. and Mrs. Griffin,
I called you in today

01:45.095 --> 01:46.595
because your son, Chris,
has been getting

01:46.630 --> 01:48.298
in quite a bit of
trouble lately.

01:48.332 --> 01:50.817
In fact, just yesterday,
he tried to pass

01:50.851 --> 01:54.155
this drawing off
as his Advanced Art project.

01:54.189 --> 01:56.390
Oh, my.
I'm very embarrassed.

01:56.425 --> 01:57.893
Well, someone should be.

01:57.927 --> 01:59.294
I'm sorry,
Principal Shepherd.

01:59.329 --> 02:01.230
We've tried talking
to Chris.

02:01.265 --> 02:02.899
We just don't know
what else to do.

02:02.933 --> 02:04.735
Well, I think we may
have a solution.

02:04.769 --> 02:06.870
We have these cats that
hang around the school,

02:06.905 --> 02:08.138
and we feed them.

02:08.173 --> 02:09.441
Why doesn't
Chris come in,

02:09.475 --> 02:11.526
and we can treat him
like one of the cats?

02:11.560 --> 02:13.779
And in a couple of years,
we'll give him a diploma.

02:13.814 --> 02:14.980
That doesn't
sound like

02:15.015 --> 02:16.248
he'll be getting
the best education.

02:16.283 --> 02:17.417
I don't know, Lois.

02:17.452 --> 02:19.836
Some of those cats go on
to get pretty good jobs.

02:21.172 --> 02:23.091
(meows)
Yep.

02:26.346 --> 02:28.781
<i>ANNOUNCER (over TV):
We now return to Breaking Bad.</i>

02:28.815 --> 02:30.083
(eerie whirring over TV)

02:30.117 --> 02:32.352
<i>You will recommend
Breaking Bad</i>

02:32.386 --> 02:34.121
to everyone you know.

02:34.156 --> 02:37.191
<i>I will recommend Breaking Bad
to everyone I know.</i>

02:37.225 --> 02:40.062
<i>Breaking Bad is the best show
you've ever seen,</i>

02:40.096 --> 02:41.797
<i>except maybe The Wire.</i>

02:41.831 --> 02:44.500
<i>Breaking Bad
is the best show I've ever seen,</i>

02:44.535 --> 02:46.170
<i>except maybe The Wire.</i>

02:46.204 --> 02:47.604
You will never stop talking

02:47.639 --> 02:49.907
<i>about Breaking Bad
or The Wire.</i>

02:49.941 --> 02:53.912
<i>I will never stop talking
about Breaking Bad or The Wire.</i>

02:53.946 --> 02:55.998
Peter, I'm really
worried about Chris.

02:56.032 --> 02:57.917
What are we going
to do about him?

02:59.587 --> 03:00.654
What the hell happened

03:00.688 --> 03:02.355
to all the smart genes
in this family?

03:02.390 --> 03:04.208
I mean, I invent
the razor blade comb,

03:04.259 --> 03:05.560
and my kids
are doing nothing.

03:05.594 --> 03:07.863
I mean, when Chris grows
up, will he even be able

03:07.897 --> 03:09.998
to get a job or take
care of himself?

03:10.033 --> 03:12.235
And what's he going
to do when we die?

03:14.338 --> 03:17.074
♪ Ooga chaka, ooga ooga,
ooga chaka... ♪

03:17.109 --> 03:20.312
♪ I can't stop
this feeling ♪

03:20.346 --> 03:24.050
♪ Deep inside
of me... ♪

03:24.084 --> 03:25.685
I feel awful
saying it,

03:25.719 --> 03:28.088
but I'm kind of embarrassed
by him sometimes.

03:28.123 --> 03:29.990
I know, and he's
got boobs, too.

03:30.024 --> 03:31.593
Stupid guy with boobs.

03:31.627 --> 03:33.311
Acting like he
doesn't want us to look.

03:33.345 --> 03:34.646
He wants us to look.

03:34.680 --> 03:36.015
CHRIS:
Stop making fun of me!

03:36.049 --> 03:37.183
(both gasp)

03:37.217 --> 03:39.585
I just wanted to listen
to you guys have sex,

03:39.619 --> 03:42.255
but you were saying
mean things about me.

03:42.290 --> 03:43.557
Chris!
Oh, my God.

03:43.591 --> 03:45.710
No, we weren't
talking about you.

03:45.744 --> 03:47.295
Yeah, we were talking
about Meg.

03:47.329 --> 03:49.198
We call her Chris
so she doesn't know.

03:49.232 --> 03:51.133
MEG: (bleep) you!
Shut up, Chris!

03:51.167 --> 03:52.936
I know that's not true.

03:52.970 --> 03:54.804
(sobbing)

03:54.839 --> 03:56.072
Oh, God.

03:56.106 --> 03:57.925
Peter, he heard us
call him stupid.

03:57.959 --> 03:59.193
His own parents.

03:59.227 --> 04:00.494
What have we done?

04:00.530 --> 04:01.596
Well, you know, Lois,

04:01.631 --> 04:02.898
it's never easy
hearing the truth.

04:02.932 --> 04:04.717
Especially from
a fortune cookie.

04:08.655 --> 04:09.990
What does it
say, Peter?

04:10.024 --> 04:11.742
Uh, just something
about perseverance.

04:11.776 --> 04:13.027
What does yours say?

04:13.062 --> 04:14.929
It says I'm
very creative.

04:23.291 --> 04:26.160
Oh, by the way, there's a baby
in my preschool with HIV,

04:26.195 --> 04:27.995
and my teacher gave you
something to sign

04:28.030 --> 04:29.330
to get him kicked out.

04:29.364 --> 04:31.300
Hey, Chris!

04:31.334 --> 04:33.268
There's my big, smart son.

04:33.303 --> 04:35.271
My special guy,
my Chrissy.

04:35.306 --> 04:38.475
Sounds like someone got her
vibrator working again.

04:38.543 --> 04:39.877
Oh, look out, everyone.

04:39.911 --> 04:42.214
Double-digit IQ
joining the table.

04:42.248 --> 04:44.049
Hey, smarty, think fast.
Aah!

04:44.083 --> 04:46.135
He knows.
He knows it's hot.

04:46.169 --> 04:47.436
'Cause he's smart.

04:47.471 --> 04:49.105
I don't want
to talk to you guys.

04:49.139 --> 04:50.440
You called me
an idiot.

04:50.475 --> 04:52.476
Oh, Chris, you must
have just misheard us.

04:52.510 --> 04:53.911
I was standing
right there.

04:53.945 --> 04:55.380
How is that
possible?

04:55.414 --> 04:56.510
'Cause you're an idiot--
that's how.

04:56.535 --> 04:57.149
Peter!

04:57.150 --> 04:59.885
I'm going
to school!

04:59.920 --> 05:01.320
It's Sunday,
right?

05:01.354 --> 05:03.990
Peter, we've completely
destroyed his self-esteem.

05:04.025 --> 05:05.825
We've got to
do something.

05:05.860 --> 05:07.745
You know, Lois, if Chris is
having confidence issues,

05:07.779 --> 05:09.196
they have camps
for kids like him.

05:09.231 --> 05:10.481
What do you mean?

05:10.515 --> 05:12.234
Well, they take kids
who struggle in school

05:12.268 --> 05:13.535
and help them gain
self-confidence

05:13.570 --> 05:15.038
in a nurturing
environment.

05:15.072 --> 05:17.307
Wow. That's actually
a great idea.

05:17.341 --> 05:18.791
We don't got
no money for camp.

05:18.826 --> 05:20.294
We're saving up
to go to Europe.

05:20.328 --> 05:22.429
Peter, we went to Europe.
You didn't like it.

05:22.464 --> 05:24.015
Oh, my God, it's beautiful.

05:24.050 --> 05:25.984
My TV isn't here.
Let's go home.

05:29.456 --> 05:32.326
<i>ANNOUNCER (over TV): We now return
to Canadian Horror Story.</i>

05:32.360 --> 05:34.294
Hey, hon, we're
out of Molson.

05:34.329 --> 05:35.562
Oh, not to worry.

05:35.597 --> 05:37.265
We've got some Labatt's
in the garage.

05:37.299 --> 05:39.033
And could you get one
for the ghost?

05:39.068 --> 05:40.352
Boo, eh?

05:40.387 --> 05:42.922
Hey, Chris,
could we talk to you?

05:42.956 --> 05:45.558
If you're going to insult me
again, just go away.

05:45.593 --> 05:47.160
Look, honey, we're sorry,

05:47.194 --> 05:49.063
and we want to do
something special for you.

05:49.097 --> 05:50.337
This is a Hamilton
Beach blender

05:50.349 --> 05:51.649
we got on our
wedding day.

05:51.683 --> 05:52.951
It keeps coming
back to us

05:52.986 --> 05:54.119
in a vicious
regifting cycle.

05:54.153 --> 05:55.454
No, Peter.

05:55.488 --> 05:57.357
Chris, we were thinking
of sending you to camp.

05:57.391 --> 06:00.276
And we're going to let
you choose which one.

06:00.311 --> 06:02.279
This one's called
Camp Helmet.

06:02.314 --> 06:04.683
This one's called
Camp Sit 'N' Stay.

06:04.717 --> 06:06.718
These camps
don't sound fun at all.

06:06.752 --> 06:09.055
Well, Chris, camp is only
as fun as you make it.

06:09.089 --> 06:11.090
That's how it was
in World War II.

06:12.125 --> 06:13.327
Don't suppose
it would help

06:13.361 --> 06:15.329
to say I have a note
from my doctor.

06:15.363 --> 06:17.665
(laughs)
Get in there, you.

06:17.700 --> 06:19.217
Hey, this one
looks cool.

06:19.251 --> 06:20.551
Space Camp.

06:20.586 --> 06:23.188
"Spend a week doing
intensive math and science,

06:23.223 --> 06:24.723
learning from
real astronauts."

06:24.758 --> 06:26.026
Space Camp?

06:26.060 --> 06:27.594
I'm sorry,
that one must have

06:27.628 --> 06:29.563
accidentally gotten in
with the rest of them.

06:29.598 --> 06:31.966
What are you saying?
That I'm too stupid for it?

06:32.017 --> 06:33.084
Chris, we're over here.

06:33.118 --> 06:34.336
What are you saying?

06:34.370 --> 06:35.954
That I'm too
stupid for it?

06:35.989 --> 06:37.740
No, no,
of course not.

06:37.775 --> 06:39.976
Then this is the camp I choose.

06:40.010 --> 06:42.212
Yay! I get to go
to Space Camp!

06:42.247 --> 06:43.781
Thanks, Mom and Dad.

06:43.815 --> 06:46.401
You guys are
the best parents ever!

06:46.435 --> 06:48.603
Would you excuse
me a moment?

06:50.607 --> 06:52.124
These are mine now.

06:52.159 --> 06:53.325
Hey!

06:53.377 --> 06:54.978
Oh, honey, when we
accepted those mugs,

06:55.013 --> 06:56.513
we knew this
could happen.

07:03.926 --> 07:05.693
You know, Chris,
it's not too late

07:05.727 --> 07:07.462
to change your mind
about Space Camp.

07:07.497 --> 07:09.364
Sometimes it can be
tough to fit in

07:09.399 --> 07:10.732
with a new group
of people.

07:10.768 --> 07:12.001
Yeah, I sure
had my troubles

07:12.036 --> 07:13.937
when I got involved
with the Boy Scouts.

07:13.971 --> 07:15.539
At no time were
the campers

07:15.574 --> 07:17.641
and Mr. Griffin naked
simultaneously.

07:17.676 --> 07:19.778
They merely engaged
in horseplay.

07:19.812 --> 07:21.913
Did Mr. Griffin
sodomize the boys?

07:23.433 --> 07:26.035
Mr. Griffin vehemently
denies all allegations.

07:26.069 --> 07:27.804
Mom, I can handle
Space Camp.

07:27.839 --> 07:29.339
I'm not stupid.

07:29.374 --> 07:30.774
Well, regardless,
on the way back,

07:30.808 --> 07:33.244
we're stopping at that Down
syndrome camp we passed.

07:33.279 --> 07:35.513
Peter, that was
the University of Florida.

07:40.404 --> 07:42.472
Welcome to Space Camp.

07:42.506 --> 07:44.809
Hi. We're here to register
our son, Chris Griffin.

07:44.843 --> 07:46.677
Oh, great to
meet you, Chris.

07:46.712 --> 07:48.447
Hey, this kid means
the world to me.

07:48.481 --> 07:49.848
Keep an eye on him, huh?

07:51.935 --> 07:53.152
Hey, what's that?
I thought all

07:53.187 --> 07:54.354
the space shuttles
were decommissioned.

07:54.388 --> 07:56.357
Well, we always keep one
spacecraft flight-ready

07:56.391 --> 07:58.092
in the event a satellite
needs service,

07:58.126 --> 07:59.560
there's a space
station emergency

07:59.594 --> 08:02.297
or an Italian guy needs
to overexaggerate.

08:02.331 --> 08:04.884
I swear to God, Johnny,
I've had it up to...

08:12.427 --> 08:13.911
...here with your BS.

08:13.946 --> 08:15.880
Ooh, he's really
had it this time.

08:15.914 --> 08:17.449
I'm sorry, Louie!

08:17.484 --> 08:18.817
All right, then.

08:18.852 --> 08:20.185
Hey, what's that
in there?

08:20.220 --> 08:22.005
That's a sensory
deprivation room

08:22.023 --> 08:23.223
that prepares
our astronauts

08:23.257 --> 08:24.924
for the terrifying
emptiness of space.

08:24.959 --> 08:26.193
Oh, cool.

08:26.228 --> 08:28.829
Hey, come on, Brian,
try it with me.

08:28.864 --> 08:31.466
(Peter and Brian screaming)

08:31.501 --> 08:34.020
Things got crazy
so fast.

08:36.223 --> 08:37.256
Bye, Chris.
Good luck.

08:37.291 --> 08:38.525
Take care, Chris.

08:38.560 --> 08:41.028
Oh, and hey, if you get
sent off on a space mission,

08:41.062 --> 08:43.465
um, don't be the black guy,
'cause you'll die.

08:43.499 --> 08:44.966
I know you're going
to have fun

08:45.001 --> 08:46.502
and make lots
of good friends.

08:46.536 --> 08:47.770
Yeah, and not
fake friends,

08:47.804 --> 08:49.772
like guys
whose wives are friends.

08:49.806 --> 08:50.924
(women giggling)

08:50.958 --> 08:52.559
You have to get me
that recipe.

08:52.593 --> 08:54.094
Oh, my God,
the cookbook?

08:54.128 --> 08:56.397
It's right in
the kitchen.

08:56.432 --> 08:58.300
Sports?
No.

08:58.335 --> 09:00.235
Movies?
No.

09:00.270 --> 09:02.371
Hiking?
No.

09:02.406 --> 09:03.673
Cars?
No.

09:03.708 --> 09:04.841
Porn?
Porn?

09:04.875 --> 09:06.009
Yes!
Yes!

09:06.043 --> 09:07.378
Gay porn?
No.

09:10.582 --> 09:12.784
Welcome to Space Camp,
everyone.

09:12.819 --> 09:14.519
Be sure to grab
your physics textbooks.

09:14.554 --> 09:16.222
And you may want to brush up

09:16.256 --> 09:18.291
for tomorrow's lecture
on orbital dynamics.

09:18.325 --> 09:20.761
Oh, man. I don't belong here,
and they know it.

09:20.795 --> 09:22.896
People can always sense a fraud.

09:22.931 --> 09:24.766
Gentlemen, it has come
to my attention

09:24.800 --> 09:26.501
that there's
a Chinese among us.

09:26.535 --> 09:28.504
Rest assured, he will be found.

09:28.538 --> 09:30.439
Security is launching
a full-scale investigation

09:30.474 --> 09:31.925
into the
genealogy records

09:31.959 --> 09:33.877
of every single one
of our employees.

09:33.911 --> 09:35.328
This man will be found,

09:35.363 --> 09:37.548
and there will be
dire consequences.

09:37.583 --> 09:40.669
Too bad about
that Chinese guy, huh?

09:42.405 --> 09:43.907
Campers, we are now in

09:43.941 --> 09:45.642
our state-of-the-art
space shuttle simulator.

09:45.676 --> 09:47.577
Hey, you're a girl.
I just figured that out.

09:47.611 --> 09:49.180
By the time you
leave Space Camp,

09:49.214 --> 09:50.481
you will all be familiar

09:50.515 --> 09:52.517
with every device
on board this craft.

09:52.552 --> 09:53.952
What's this button do?

09:53.986 --> 09:55.921
Why don't you go
ahead and push it?

09:55.955 --> 09:57.724
(rock music plays
over speaker)

09:57.758 --> 09:59.358
That's my band.

10:01.696 --> 10:04.799
Okay, campers, let's see
your astronaut haircuts.

10:04.834 --> 10:06.267
Mm-hmm.

10:06.302 --> 10:08.136
Mm-hmm. Mm-hmm.

10:08.170 --> 10:09.839
Chris, that's
a Russell Brand haircut.

10:09.873 --> 10:11.507
(British accent): Oy, I don't know
what happened.

10:11.541 --> 10:13.143
I went to me barber
at the shop,

10:13.177 --> 10:14.811
and I come out,
I'm looking like this.

10:14.846 --> 10:16.713
(laughs) You're talking
different from how I talk.

10:16.748 --> 10:19.016
Here's a bunch of money
to be on TV.

10:21.020 --> 10:23.857
<i>If you all saw Apollo 13,
you know they had to fix</i>

10:23.891 --> 10:26.393
an air scrubber with just
the tools in the capsule.

10:26.428 --> 10:29.364
Griffin, I need you to use
these tools to fix my marriage.

10:29.398 --> 10:30.632
I can't do that.

10:30.666 --> 10:33.101
Well, I can't be
the only one who's trying.

10:38.760 --> 10:39.726
(phone rings)

10:39.761 --> 10:41.428
Hello?

10:41.463 --> 10:43.765
Oh, my God, Chris.
What's wrong?

10:43.799 --> 10:45.166
Mom, I hate it here.

10:45.201 --> 10:46.635
I want to come home.

10:46.670 --> 10:47.870
All the kids are mean,

10:47.904 --> 10:49.672
and I keep messing
everything up.

10:49.706 --> 10:52.042
Even Jinx the Robot
is picking on me.

10:52.076 --> 10:55.513
(robotic monotone): Hey, you've got
something on your shirt.

11:02.088 --> 11:04.224
Joke complete.

11:04.258 --> 11:05.742
You and dad were right.

11:05.776 --> 11:07.845
I'm not smart enough
to be here.

11:07.879 --> 11:09.880
Can you come get me,
please, Mom?

11:09.915 --> 11:12.150
Okay, honey, of
course we will.

11:12.185 --> 11:14.119
(phone beeps) Hang on, I got
another call.

11:14.153 --> 11:15.388
Hello?

11:15.422 --> 11:17.123
Lois, I'm at the gym.

11:17.157 --> 11:18.424
I hate it here.

11:18.458 --> 11:19.893
I want to come home.

11:19.928 --> 11:21.328
Everybody's so mean.

11:21.362 --> 11:23.598
And there's this one guy
who follows me around

11:23.632 --> 11:24.899
and calls me fat.

11:24.934 --> 11:26.868
Well, I suppose I...
(phone beeps)

11:26.902 --> 11:28.704
Hang on, honey, I'm
getting another call.

11:28.738 --> 11:29.705
Hello?

11:29.739 --> 11:30.906
Hey, is fat ass
home yet?

11:30.941 --> 11:32.626
Put him on,
I want to talk to him.

11:36.164 --> 11:38.399
Thanks for coming
to get me, you guys.

11:38.433 --> 11:39.568
Hey, no problem.

11:39.602 --> 11:41.854
We spent thousands of dollars
to send you here

11:41.888 --> 11:43.990
and made two trips
to Florida in four days.

11:44.024 --> 11:45.725
I'm glad we had you.

11:45.759 --> 11:47.912
Well, I'm sorry, I thought
I could handle it here,

11:47.946 --> 11:49.113
but I was wrong.

11:49.147 --> 11:51.515
That's okay, honey,
everyone makes mistakes.

11:51.550 --> 11:54.352
Yeah, remember when the fat man
brought home that cougar chick?

11:54.387 --> 11:55.921
You make yourself
comfortable, Beth.

11:55.955 --> 11:57.173
I'll be right back.

11:57.207 --> 11:58.341
Hi, young man.

11:58.375 --> 11:59.492
I'm looking for
a good time.

11:59.526 --> 12:00.661
Uh-huh.

12:00.695 --> 12:02.262
I'm worldly. I'm fun.

12:02.297 --> 12:04.131
This ain't mileage,
this is experience.

12:04.165 --> 12:05.266
Not interested.

12:05.301 --> 12:07.235
Listen, just so there
are no surprises,

12:07.269 --> 12:08.804
don't be scared--
my stuff looks like

12:08.838 --> 12:10.773
someone spilled an
apple pie in my lap.

12:10.807 --> 12:12.608
(groans) Yuck.

12:12.642 --> 12:14.644
Didn't you have any
fun at all, sweetie?

12:14.646 --> 12:17.281
Well, I guess I did get to go
on the space shuttle everyday.

12:17.316 --> 12:18.549
Really? No way.

12:18.584 --> 12:21.353
I always wanted to be an
astronaut like Lance Armstrong.

12:21.404 --> 12:23.188
It's Neil Armstrong.

12:23.222 --> 12:25.291
He lied about his name, too?

12:30.782 --> 12:32.816
Wow, this
is amazing, Chris.

12:32.851 --> 12:33.818
Look at this, Lois.

12:33.853 --> 12:35.320
a week ago,
we call him stupid,

12:35.354 --> 12:37.455
and now he's giving tours
of the space shuttle.

12:37.491 --> 12:39.592
Negative reinforcement--
that's the key.

12:39.626 --> 12:40.760
Right, stupid?

12:40.794 --> 12:42.763
Ow! I'll
do better.

12:42.797 --> 12:44.898
So, do you know how
all this stuff works?

12:44.933 --> 12:46.718
Well, I don't know what
most of these buttons do,

12:46.752 --> 12:49.470
but I know this big red one
is the one you press to launch.

12:49.505 --> 12:50.773
Whoo, big red button!

12:50.807 --> 12:51.824
Stewie, no!

12:51.858 --> 12:53.542
(beeping)

12:53.576 --> 12:55.729
(computer whirring)

12:55.780 --> 12:57.347
(engines rumbling)
Uh-oh.

12:57.365 --> 12:58.566
Chris, make it stop.

12:58.617 --> 12:59.734
I'm trying.

13:02.538 --> 13:05.874
(engines roaring)

13:05.908 --> 13:08.127
I think...
I think we're launching.

13:08.162 --> 13:10.380
Oh, my God, everyone
strap yourselves in.

13:10.414 --> 13:11.498
Stewie, hold my hand.

13:11.532 --> 13:13.083
No, thank you,
I prefer to die

13:13.134 --> 13:14.368
giving you the finger.

13:14.386 --> 13:17.389
If this is what it takes
to get out of Florida, fine.

13:21.011 --> 13:22.561
You know what,
you only live once.

13:22.596 --> 13:24.314
I am sticking my head
out the window.

13:24.349 --> 13:26.984
Yes, this is the best version

13:27.018 --> 13:29.404
of whatever's so enjoyable
about this.

13:35.387 --> 13:36.820
Mom, I'm scared.

13:36.855 --> 13:37.922
I don't believe it.

13:37.956 --> 13:39.156
Are we really
stuck in space?

13:39.190 --> 13:41.376
Oh, God, what if we
have to live up here

13:41.410 --> 13:42.911
for the rest
of our lives?

13:42.945 --> 13:44.447
I'm gonna miss
drinking at The Clam.

13:44.481 --> 13:45.761
I'll tell you what I won't miss.

13:45.782 --> 13:47.717
That waiter who thinks
he has to be funny.

13:47.751 --> 13:49.536
Yes, I'd like the
chopped salad, please,

13:49.571 --> 13:51.338
and could I get
no onions on that?

13:51.372 --> 13:54.208
Hmm, I've got a few connections
in the back.

13:54.243 --> 13:55.543
I'll see what I can do.

13:55.578 --> 13:56.679
Yeah, that's funny.

13:56.713 --> 13:58.347
If there's one onion
on that salad,

13:58.381 --> 14:00.984
you can forget the $1.80
tip you need to live.

14:01.018 --> 14:03.887
I'm sorry, you guys,
this is all my fault.

14:03.921 --> 14:06.157
I never should've brought you
on the space shuttle.

14:06.191 --> 14:08.594
You were right--
I am stupid.

14:08.628 --> 14:10.829
MAN (over radio): Hello, up there.
Hello?

14:10.863 --> 14:13.166
I know you all must be pretty
terrified right now,

14:13.200 --> 14:14.634
but we're doing everything
in our power

14:14.668 --> 14:16.336
to get you safely back to earth.

14:16.370 --> 14:18.539
We're pretty good at this--
we're like six for eight.

14:18.573 --> 14:20.207
Is there anything
we can do?

14:20.241 --> 14:22.577
Well, fortunately
the ship runs on autopilot.

14:22.612 --> 14:24.579
We just need some time to figure
out your coordinates,

14:24.614 --> 14:26.215
and calculate the reentry plan.

14:26.249 --> 14:28.651
So, just try to stay calm
and we'll figure this all out.

14:28.685 --> 14:31.021
By the way,
you're all quite famous now.

14:31.055 --> 14:32.623
You're on every news channel.

14:32.657 --> 14:34.292
Good afternoon,
I'm Brian Williams.

14:34.326 --> 14:37.261
Our top story, an accidental
space shuttle launch

14:37.296 --> 14:39.832
that has sent a Rhode Island
family into orbit.

14:39.866 --> 14:42.102
For more on the story,
we go to our local affiliate

14:42.136 --> 14:44.638
Channel Five Quahog where
Tom Tucker is on the scene.

14:44.672 --> 14:45.639
Tom?

14:45.674 --> 14:46.841
Thanks, Brian.

14:46.875 --> 14:48.743
I, uh, saw that you opened
my birthday Evite.

14:48.777 --> 14:51.279
Now I just need you
to go ahead and reply.

14:51.314 --> 14:53.415
I'm standing here at the home
of the Griffin family,

14:53.449 --> 14:54.684
chatting with their neighbors.

14:54.718 --> 14:56.352
So, the Griffins
might be dead.

14:56.387 --> 14:57.520
How does that
make you feel?

14:57.554 --> 15:00.207
Griffin dead?
I take.

15:02.845 --> 15:04.346
No, no, they
might be dead.

15:04.380 --> 15:05.947
They also might
still be alive.

15:05.981 --> 15:07.950
It's okay, I-I take.

15:07.984 --> 15:09.985
(loud rumbling)

15:11.856 --> 15:13.457
(loud popping)

15:18.397 --> 15:19.982
Peter, what are you doing?

15:20.016 --> 15:21.633
I'm going out for
a space walk, Lois.

15:21.668 --> 15:23.119
You know, this
is our one chance

15:23.137 --> 15:25.255
to experience the
awe-inspiring wonders

15:25.289 --> 15:27.006
of space firsthand.

15:30.946 --> 15:33.215
Wow, neat.

15:33.249 --> 15:35.250
So, this is space.

15:42.177 --> 15:44.162
Damn it.

15:45.915 --> 15:48.617
(girl laughing)

15:48.651 --> 15:51.287
Ha! Stupid drunk bitches.

15:53.741 --> 15:54.875
Hey, Brian, check it out.

15:54.909 --> 15:56.644
Meg's poking
around the cargo hold.

15:56.678 --> 15:58.546
I'm gonna give her
a little scare.

15:59.865 --> 16:01.083
(screams)

16:09.010 --> 16:10.144
(sniffs)

16:10.178 --> 16:12.581
Aw, boy, I think
this space food has gone bad.

16:12.615 --> 16:14.332
Peter, that's a
fecal collection bag.

16:14.367 --> 16:15.567
Oh, there's a bag for that?

16:15.618 --> 16:17.203
'Cause mine's over there
twirling in the air

16:17.237 --> 16:18.721
like a slow baton.

16:18.756 --> 16:21.708
STEWIE: Well, someone's got to lead
this marching band. Ew!

16:21.743 --> 16:22.960
MAN (over radio):
Griffins come in.

16:22.994 --> 16:24.728
Yes! Yes, we're here.

16:24.763 --> 16:26.264
Well, I have some good news.

16:26.298 --> 16:29.033
The autopilot is capable
of taking you home.

16:29.069 --> 16:30.302
But guys...
(static crackling)

16:30.337 --> 16:31.771
...to engage, we're gonna...

16:31.805 --> 16:33.306
...to take the shuttle out of...

16:33.341 --> 16:34.975
(radio goes silent)

16:35.009 --> 16:36.143
Hello?
(static drones)

16:36.177 --> 16:38.312
Hello?
Crap, I think we lost him.

16:38.347 --> 16:39.647
Let me fix that.

16:39.681 --> 16:42.234
If radio deejays have taught me
anything about radio,

16:42.268 --> 16:43.569
it's that you got
to do two things.

16:43.603 --> 16:45.772
Turn it up and rip the knob off.

16:45.823 --> 16:47.557
Peter, no!
(crackling)

16:47.591 --> 16:49.476
Damn it, now we've lost
communication entirely.

16:49.510 --> 16:50.711
What are we gonna do?

16:50.745 --> 16:52.329
I don't know,
but we got to try something.

16:52.364 --> 16:53.864
If we don't get this shuttle
out of orbit soon,

16:53.899 --> 16:55.667
we're gonna
run out of oxygen and die.

16:55.701 --> 16:56.818
Is failure an option?

16:56.853 --> 16:58.003
No, it's not.

16:58.038 --> 16:59.539
Aw, that was my suggestion.

16:59.573 --> 17:01.274
All right, come on, everyone,
we got to put our heads

17:01.308 --> 17:02.543
together
and try to find a solution.

17:02.577 --> 17:04.411
Don't worry-- Griffins
have a long history

17:04.445 --> 17:05.579
of figuring stuff out.

17:05.613 --> 17:06.915
Like my great-grandfather

17:06.949 --> 17:08.716
who invented the
first marital aid.

17:11.354 --> 17:13.556
This gives me an idea.

17:16.561 --> 17:17.894
She's dead.

17:20.933 --> 17:22.233
All right, come on,
if mission control thought

17:22.267 --> 17:23.602
we could help get the shuttle
out of orbit,

17:23.620 --> 17:24.803
it can't be that hard.

17:24.838 --> 17:26.154
Okay, okay,
uh, try this.

17:26.206 --> 17:27.707
Hit up, up, down, down,

17:27.741 --> 17:29.959
left, right, left, right,
B, A, start.

17:29.994 --> 17:31.245
Then we'll have
unlimited lives.

17:31.279 --> 17:32.997
Come on,
we're running out of time.

17:33.048 --> 17:35.149
Oh, Meg, all your suicide
threats over the years,

17:35.183 --> 17:37.952
and you're just as chicken
(bleep) as the rest of us.

17:37.970 --> 17:40.840
Hey, guys, I think I have
an idea that could help us.

17:40.874 --> 17:43.359
In Space Camp, we learned about
countering forward momentum

17:43.393 --> 17:44.978
with retro rockets.

17:45.013 --> 17:47.614
If we use them now,
the ship might slow down enough

17:47.650 --> 17:50.301
for gravity
to pull us out of orbit.

17:50.336 --> 17:53.355
PETER: That's an egg that I
hucked at you for being a nerd.

17:55.325 --> 17:57.244
Chris, if you think it'll help,
just try it!

18:01.166 --> 18:03.634
Ah, we're slowing down.
That's a good sign.

18:03.669 --> 18:05.637
<i>You know, what's not slowing
down-- Breaking Bad.</i>

18:05.672 --> 18:07.856
<i>Haven't seen anything like it
since The Wire.</i>

18:07.891 --> 18:09.876
God, he never shuts up
about those shows.

18:09.910 --> 18:11.010
Oh, my God!

18:11.045 --> 18:12.829
I think it might be working!

18:12.847 --> 18:14.331
(screaming)

18:14.349 --> 18:15.999
BRIAN:
We're spinning out!

18:18.854 --> 18:20.438
MEG: Oh, my God,
we're gonna die!

18:20.473 --> 18:22.058
There's so many things
I didn't do.

18:22.109 --> 18:23.693
I never got to go to college!

18:23.727 --> 18:26.280
My book wasn't all
I wanted it to be in spots!

18:26.314 --> 18:28.515
Peter, I wanted to
grow old with you!

18:28.533 --> 18:30.068
Well, you kind of did.

18:30.119 --> 18:32.103
I never got to wear it better!

18:35.875 --> 18:38.861
(grunts) I have to reorient
the retro rockets

18:38.879 --> 18:41.081
to counter the spin rotation!

18:43.001 --> 18:44.468
(grunts)

19:01.407 --> 19:02.558
Chris, what are you doing?

19:02.576 --> 19:04.226
(dramatically):
Saving our lives.

19:04.244 --> 19:05.911
Aw, I wish he
hadn't said that.

19:05.946 --> 19:07.614
Now I'm kind of
rooting against him.

19:11.170 --> 19:13.037
We're straightening out!

19:14.574 --> 19:16.458
Ah, look! It says
"autopilot engaged."

19:16.509 --> 19:18.962
Good for him.
I'm glad he found someone.

19:18.996 --> 19:20.730
Oh, thank God,
we're saved!

19:20.765 --> 19:24.235
I can't believe I got to go
to space before Derek Watson.

19:24.269 --> 19:25.636
Who's Derek Watson?

19:25.671 --> 19:27.222
Nobody now.

19:42.875 --> 19:44.710
They've re-entered
the atmosphere!

19:44.745 --> 19:47.246
(cheering)

19:47.280 --> 19:49.833
I'm not cheering.
I don't even know them.

19:58.011 --> 19:59.979
(tires screech)

20:00.014 --> 20:03.016
(cheering)

20:11.928 --> 20:14.580
(cheering,
camera shutter clicking)

20:16.751 --> 20:18.702
Well, thanks to
no gravity in space,

20:18.753 --> 20:21.790
my spine stretched and I am
now a confident six, three,

20:21.824 --> 20:24.810
so, Lois, I will be leaving you
for a hotter woman.

20:24.845 --> 20:27.580
Never mind, Lois,
I love you so much.

20:31.085 --> 20:34.122
Chris, our family is alive
right now because of you.

20:34.156 --> 20:35.389
You saved us.

20:35.424 --> 20:36.574
That's right, buddy.

20:36.608 --> 20:38.644
It was your brains
that pulled us through.

20:38.678 --> 20:40.963
We're so sorry
we ever doubted you.

20:40.997 --> 20:42.198
Thanks, you guys.

20:42.233 --> 20:43.366
You bet, champ.

20:43.400 --> 20:45.503
Hey, by the way,
who moved our house?

20:45.504 --> 21:46.504
Thank you for watching «Family Guy» on
www.FOX-fan.ru